"@@@ If the index is greater than or equal to 0 and less than or equal to the initial size of the vector, then for every valid index i from the insertion index to the old size of the vector, the element at position i+1 in the updated vector is either null and the old element at i was null, or it is equal to the old element at i."

Interpretation: Preconditions: index >=0 && index <= old size (size before insertion). Then for all int i; i >= index && i < oldSize; the element at position i+1 after insertion (i.e., this.elementAt(i+1) after) satisfies: (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) . Since element may be null, we need to compare with null.

We need observer methods: elementAt(int) returns E, get(int) also returns element. Use this.elementAt maybe accessible. In spec we can use this.elementAt(i+1). For old value: \old(this.elementAt(i)). For null equality we can use == null. For object equality we can use .equals but need to avoid null pointer. Could use (this.elementAt(i+1) == null ? \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null : this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))).

Simplify: (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || (this.elementAt(i+1) != null && this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))).

But we can use the equals approach with null safe: java.util.Objects.equals(a,b) but not available as observer? Might be allowed to call static method? Usually spec only uses observers; but we can use .equals with null check.

We'll produce assert:

assert (index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; i >= index && i < \old(this.size()); ( (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || (this.elementAt(i+1) != null && this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) );

Need to get old size: \old(this.size()).

Make sure parentheses.

Thus final answer code block.

