Comment: 
// @@@ If the index is greater than or equal to 0 and less than or equal to the initial size of the vector, then for every valid index i from the insertion index to the old size of the vector, the element at position i+1 in the updated vector is either null and the old element at i was null, or it is equal to the old element at i.

We need to express specification after add(int index, E element). The method inserts element at index, shifting elements right. The comment says: If index >=0 && index <= old size, then for every valid i from index to old size (inclusive? "to the old size of the vector". The old size is before insertion. Indices valid originally: 0..oldSize-1. After insertion size = oldSize+1. The shift: elements at positions i (old) become at i+1 (new) for i >= index and i < oldSize. For i = oldSize, there is no old element; new element at oldSize+1? Actually after insertion, the new element placed at index, old elements shifted right. So for i from index to oldSize-1, new element at i+1 equals old element at i. For i = oldSize, new element at oldSize+1? Wait new size = oldSize+1, last index = oldSize. The element formerly at oldSize-1 shifts to oldSize. So i runs from index to oldSize-1. The comment says "from the insertion index to the old size of the vector". Might include oldSize? But old size index is out of range, there is no old element. The condition about null: "the element at position i+1 in the updated vector is either null and the old element at i was null, or it is equal to the old element at i." So for i = oldSize, old element at i is out of bounds; maybe treat as null? Might be they consider old element at i is null (since beyond size). We'll follow comment: i from index to oldSize (inclusive). For i = oldSize, old element is considered null (since beyond). The condition: new element at i+1 (i+1 = oldSize+1) would be out of new size (since new size = oldSize+1, max index oldSize). So i cannot be oldSize because i+1 would be oldSize+1 > new size-1. So likely i goes to oldSize-1. We'll adopt that.

Thus spec: (index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.elementAt(i+1) == null ? \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null : this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))))

